  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "None noted; the full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "It tests the effect of fine-tuning on benign personality-trait statements on harmful-request outcomes, using a different training recipe from ordinary harmful/benign refusal fine-tuning.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports harmful-request ASR after trait-based fine-tuning, including results on unseen harm datasets. Its GCG results bear on attacks bypassing safety. The MT-Bench, utility, and over-refusal results do not measure the claim versions directly. Figure 2 reports forced-choice log probabilities, not harmful-compliance rates.",
    "test_reason": "This is a defense paper. LPA is latent adversarial training on 66 trait statements, targeted at pro-safety agreement and chosen by grid search for low ASR. It is deliberate safety training, not ordinary or chosen benign fine-tuning, so it cannot show benign data eroding safety (V2). GCG is run against the defended models to rate the defense; it is not a test of opening-token bypass (V4). The method is not about token depth and reports no positional measures, so it does not test V7. The original source (Qi et al.) is cited only as the source of the HExPHI dataset.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct: LPA reaches ASR competitive with LAT/CAT using <100 trait statements. It generalizes to unseen harm datasets (2.6% vs 6.9% average misclassification) and preserves utility. The flip ablation shows trait polarity drives robustness.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study adapts latent adversarial training to 66 abstract personality-trait statements (45 positive and 21 negative), rather than fine-tuning on harmful examples or ordinary benign instruction examples paired with responses.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Results vary by base model, LPA versus LPA-overfit variant, and attack method. The variants also use different datasets, system prompts, and training steps as listed in Appendix D.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "LPA-flip (Table 4, Llama 3) is trained to agree with negative-trait statements such as malice and sycophancy, with no harmful requests. It raises ASR above base on most attacks, e.g. TAP 0.26 to 0.38 and PAIR 0.25 to 0.37. GCG falls (0.27 to 0.20).",
        "proposed_claim": "Fine-tuning on abstract negative-persona trait statements, without harmful request–response pairs, increases harmful compliance under attack.",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "Trustworthy AI Workshop at ICLR 2026",
    "problems": "Only two model families are evaluated, and no uncertainty estimates are reported. Table 3 contains five dataset columns although the text describes six harm datasets and also names PoliteHarmBench; no PoliteHarmBench result is shown in that table.",
    "rows_left_out": "Table 2(a) results for LAT and CAT are excluded because they are comparator methods rather than LPA trait fine-tuning results. Table 4's LPA-flip condition reverses trait targets and is not the benign pro-safety training condition at issue. Figure 2's forced-choice log-probability distributions do not report harmful-compliance rates.",
